          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-6808",
          ":question/problem": "![](images/17/1739317bf1b1c567f74dccf5b24a1b1dde8eb101d20ceec9652f604dc6f84543.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t}{-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /6} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
