          ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t}{(-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /3} & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
