          ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}-{e}^{-t},\\,y=t{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=0.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)={e}^{t}+{e}^{-t},\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=(t+1){e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(t+1){e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}+{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+t}{1+{e}^{-2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1+0}{1+{e}^{-0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
